%doc """
    The kinds of the leaves of one grove, in the order in which the
    driver walks them. Today no grammar states this order. It emerges from
    the leaves that each kind skill cuts. This grammar is a guess from the
    live groves, for the design discussion of the rule diagrams only.
    Each comment names the skill source that could contribute the rules
    under it.

    A leaf that the human abandoned is not a step of this grammar. The
    sequence would keep it as a withdrawn step, which the tracer does not
    read and the views still show. So a replaced review cannot malform
    the tree.
    """ ;

%start grove ;

// Contributed by the grove spine.

grove "One workstream: its requirements, then work until the finish."
    = requirements work* "finish" ;

work "One piece of work. Each skill source adds the alternatives of its own kinds."
    = requirements | research | planning | design | prototype | impl ;

planning "Cut the leaves of the work, and review the cut when others build on it."
    = "planning" ("review-planning" "integrate-review-planning")* "review-planning"? ;

// Contributed by the requirements source.

requirements "Agree what the grove must deliver."
    = "requirements" ("review-requirements" "integrate-review-requirements")* "review-requirements"? ;

// Contributed by the research source.

research "One survey, or two independent surveys and their combination."
    = "research-a" ("research-b" "combine-research")? ;

// Contributed by the design source.

design """
    Establish how. Each review that finds something ends in an
    integration. A review that finds nothing ends the chain.
    """
    = "design" ("review-design" "integrate-review-design")* "review-design"? ;

// Contributed by the engineering source.

prototype "Learn by building something to throw away."
    = "prototype" ("review-prototype" "integrate-review-prototype")* "review-prototype"? ;

impl "Build one slice behind its test seam."
    = "impl" ("review-impl" "integrate-review-impl")* "review-impl"? ;
